ve\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333271/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333271"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333289/selection/1b83e229b382033c4386fc4f5fc4761d2b58004e62ec2f97362fedf027b62f42" :answer/id #uuid "1236868f-f743-5dbe-91c7-8a4dd5b7f95a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247f