n/1b83e229b382033c4386fc4f5fc4761d2b58004e62ec2f97362fedf027b62f42" "q-333289/selection/d917c5607292465b88630fa935554cf051a52a6553b1b405f81dc633bdbe31ac" "q-333289/selection/931aea7c7b3cb46c24869c1b115039040f5cb1d6974e2d64e9018fe9a9043ef5" "q-333289/selection/7774dbcad28ba782b825b9785c5b1dbad0851e2d71894227f969bc34b38bb723" "q-333289/selection/82fa4d2fced757dcebd43c37d2138c49527444cb07c75bd54a40d2b23db13846"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333289" :question/id #uuid "0896853c-0bff-50e3-98cf-a41d92297c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-333289" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Develop