sure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332743/field-8/0135fb78cd3e94a68911506d8f8e792e5a15d84bf9b79e74bccf3eb124eba605" :answer/id #uuid "273d16f5-3ef7-5c56-b39e-79e6f716dd2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.03" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332743/field-8/153d3b9b05a3243a639d0ca1d2044e16baa95a0952f2f1f81d42413a23c02c0a" :answer/id #uuid "6735b094-dd94-5751-96dc-e0c45cb4cbf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332743/field-8/d6f2f29582d4034bf5519fae7c54d2d72201c3db11785ffc00ab87fcfaa83907" :answer/id #uuid "979c946d-0b4e-56f2-80eb-d98d46f36654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332743/field-8/c09c96cb115612aa0f25bd798853051489aa0767b70032a0171a432d1eabb850" :answer/id #uuid "a0f86f3f-13c8-5774-8053-f7fa1a81f925" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332743/field-8" :answer-field/id #uuid "edeeedb7-74ac-57ba-8f09-f7ef97dbd112" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-332743/field-8/153d3b9b05a3243a639d0ca1d2044e16baa95a0952f2f1f81d42413a23c02c0a" :answer-field/choices ["q-332743/field-8/0135fb78cd3e94a68911506d8f8e792e5a15d84bf9b79e74bccf3eb124eba605" "q-332743/field-8/153d3b9b05a3243a639d0ca1d2044e16baa95a0952f2f1f81d42413a23c02c0a" "q-332743/field-8/d6f2f29582d4034bf5519fae7c54d2d72201c3db11785ffc00ab87fcfaa83907" "q-332743/field-8/c09c96cb115612aa0f25bd798853051489aa0767b70032a0171a432d1eabb850"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332743"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[2,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332743/field-2/ma-v1/2adcaf7e2ddc2d9aaf8643727f96e1d79afcb814c2bd041c53a4608130957719" "q-332743/field-3" "q-332743/field-4" "q-332743/field-5" "q-332743/field-6" "q-332743/field-7" "q-332743/field-8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300/field-1/0b4fc2c65192b2cc173c4e8ad58547377955072334ed0e3f1143e195e81e3110" :answer/id #uuid "f392b40e-7bb1-5b54-abbe-b3f6869e1e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300/field-1/ac225e037427ea7fdc6f7e879ac709452f853ca3432bb046292aaaf20fbd5565" :answer/id #uuid "e9a0072c-fa45-5a54-9467-b7973fc07b56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300/field-1/056e179f78ccfcc3e573146fcc7c182f8abc46e128ecf97df2624ccd64163ef9" :answer/id #uuid "3b7edf28-79ce-5d95-99e6-1749b04897a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t{e}^{3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300/field-1/74fcf99af759e31fac89d6eb8f56f394b7f994a3bb47960227b868893fc6021d" :answer/id #uuid "e627b0b4-25d8-5d2e-ad48-e2bd5b5752ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t{e}^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5abf3e04-df2a-525b-b0c6-31baf754557a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field