4a897b" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333300/field-8/2e87a24a01de4df4c5084615e733d737d26bf1cd3fe678410da7aff8cbf45301" :answer-field/choices ["q-333300/field-8/e39cff96704a0d69356d02d3c63a221633678289ca4d5ae7fa2bedd21ff776d2" "q-333300/field-8/9e0ecd3ac52ff99368669f5f34617bf0346b508d0e5368e317a2e814e87142d0" "q-333300/field-8/2e87a24a01de4df4c5084615e733d737d26bf1cd3fe678410da7aff8cbf45301" "q-333300/field-8/e679066f56f1d42037c3e4c24d08cc759c92efc5d3b26dab84533a5644114069"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333300" :question/id #uuid "ce2b17fe-4288-536e-8944-f1f7021c8fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333300" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333300/field-1" "q-333300/field-2" "q-333300/field-3" "q-333300/field-4" "q-333300/field-5" "q-333300/field-6" "q-333300/field-7" "q-333300/field-8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333300"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer/id #uuid "dfcaf4a7-65d1-589a-806a-179a033c0172" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "e0b78437-994d-5b52-aa3b-5197da204d99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "799b8c32-8b08-5134-881c-a8647ad66cb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333620/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer-field/choices ["q-333620/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" "q-333620/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer/id #uuid "7a9a1f79-eaf4-5baf-ad0b-542ca3db00a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "degenerate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" :answer/id #uuid "a9f0e04e-eb87-5154-8dee-36bf47c24853" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "star" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c55bb584-d423-56e8-91a5-823a5722ff95" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333620/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer-field/choices ["q-333620/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" "q-333620/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333620" :question/id #uuid "63439e51-31c1-5094-81c0-848ff631034c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333620" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/97a85038b240896686ef1a796d81acc6aa52d01627f3aeca4268a309badc4867.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333620/field-1" "q-333620/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333620"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer/id #uuid "21698a4a-ce37-56f1-9145-6c69dc8d6ad6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "5cc378b4-ef20-5284-bd60-e8a14f34b6af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "22b298fa-1905-5afe-b12b-9eda9a1f16f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333614/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-333614/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" "q-333614/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" :answer/id #uuid "d8f6fe01-3a69-5e14-8995-87e06fbd708e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "star" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer/id #uuid "7f02339f-fe59-5997-a123-f102f90db2b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "degenerate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4432acba-7160-5e46-9e01-ba4227671415" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333614/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer-field/choices ["q-333614/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" "q-333614/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333614" :question/id #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" :question/math-academy-id "q-333614" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333614/field-1" "q-333614/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164479 :answer-field/correct 17592186164481 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164476 :answer-field/correct 17592186164478 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333672"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =1$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/0e2180fe05500bcff2d0a1e2c066e2364eea0fd481a63866578a07e8f46b3825.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "7034c71e-207d-5746-8515-2460a66725ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer/id #uuid "91845aeb-e718-5857-81cf-884338f669f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "63e96efa-9b32-547f-b9e6-c47dee208e8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333678/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-333678/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" "q-333678/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" :answer/id #uuid "9d94ef7b-fb49-5bf8-bd35-0b29706e3f53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "star" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer/id #uuid "255e295b-34bc-53e6-83f9-ec75ff91c9aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "degenerate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5482655d-4276-5f55-93ba-cee24919bef8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333678/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" :answer-field/choices ["q-333678/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" "q-333678/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333678" :question/id #uuid "13d1d15c-2bcc-581d-b50d-d0762931b97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333678" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-1$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/92efcce675a436ad349ca6139cb12cda88fbdd5bdeaf2a5f1e755e7ba1f6d037.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333678/field-1" "q-333678/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333678"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "b9e9d94a-9c32-5e54-a952-0843fca317b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer/id #uuid "7088aecc-a224-5b7d-9d48-30e5daaa0bdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "47469da9-41cf-5677-8413-e530d20ba356" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333619/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer-field/choices ["q-333619/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" "q-333619/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" :answer/id #uuid "ca5d7f71-9768-5ed0-8c4a-bf430f88ec49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "star" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer/id #uuid "bec33e8c-e0aa-57f6-9e69-4c0f3066b506" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "degenerate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "3477023a-4e51-596b-a5ab-8fddb81eee93" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333619/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8" :answer-field/choices ["q-333619/field-2/3be1fb00a9f9d82ac8c8f67ebf8b0626813165e060c328c873db1e0eea9ec9ed" "q-333619/field-2/8fa0af88790c1f5681c0d96cf70fa80c404c283e8350cbb6abc48ff6a5888ee8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333619" :question/id #uuid "f5ad5837-4d1c-5608-be4c-e4bf0af78b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-333619" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/21b115b90b81f2f1bf3ecad3c8e200dae0ccd50c84459e047297e70e22efcb6f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333619/field-1" "q-333619/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333619"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21642"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/01d652156896744699e5533725f537e9f203031fab369accc10c912839ebad11.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/2dff913b6a0163c9dff6e28d81690472f043de69e4796dffb58b16b933165450.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c3fe70f694806cdfa2bb64064e3e97e8142c05c249def67cd22e5c21244a233b.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/3fd44b389c505756db88609e4ed7b78cdc6a2edeeb087edf3ed20034a9c909cf.png)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/fe0e0ebbdd9f7cd29ca5d2ca7633f829e682057024ba6c783fe7e10106a0bbfd.png) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/0a6ee4e5731289a20e011783360e0b17aa8e425995e13ee34950efd31cf73922.png)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/a30588ff9f9c3f7db4b7840afddf97a38df27ef7cdef48f4a3037e76836cb6a1.png)\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4c337f7f6b80f340859844e8217525db98230660d22e760c70218f0c282d21e9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21643"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/15e5c3f06172ad8218375d20178b96a80de122300761fa4a615aca099d13c52a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/daa87299ff267ce47cf44b06b218f533de30e780b12905d0e0a1888a50893dd0.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/869e6bf67c0a5826b8d29130e8bf2107674508a103c4fb5a3d7d70ab78577090.png)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/523dccf73169d02b6bdf4297e96ec84bcc6c43213cdd722000f3a987a534b218.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4fc6300682b6e28d47e43802aba9427e29c606fc6c4f77edad874e7c0e4e2c01.png)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6c84cd04f3b037bee48d02d3183114e2668fbbd110bd44e9311e7ff454db8bac.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21645"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/7d16f04f098fc32370423b1b7adaa84c14b92291bde82672b65977b0498ec011.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
